@Chalarangelo do NOT put require statements in functions. It will lead to a lot of overhead that in the worse case scenario could lead to disk access if the module has been fully garbage collected and de-instanced between each use. A require statement in node actually hits a cache algorithm that is very slow. Hence why requires should only be done once and kept around.

If the module in node has been designed badly it can lead to a worse case scenario of a memory leak. DRY coding practices should be adhered to as much as possible. Some good resources on the subject include Vitaly's write up on it in pg-promise and the bluebirdjs write up on it. More information can also be found in the node best practices repo, etc.

Requires and imports are almost analogous, so same applies there

@skatcat31 fails for falsy values

```js
dig = (obj, target) =>
  target in obj 
    ? obj[target] 
    : Object
        .values(obj)
        .reduce((acc, val) => {
          if (acc !== undefined) return acc;
          if (typeof val === 'object') {
            return dig(val, target);
          }
        }, undefined);
```

